<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Arwad</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 BCE – 64 BCE (approx.) · capital Arwad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Arwad, a tiny Phoenician island city off the Syrian coast whose sailors made it a naval power for more than a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arwad occupies a small island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tartus</a>, giving it a sheltered harbour and natural defences. A Canaanite town by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, it appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, and after 1200 BCE it became the northernmost of the great Phoenician cities, controlling the facing coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled with a council of elders over a city of tall houses and a fleet of merchant ships. Arwad supplied ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, and its king Mattan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> sent troops to the coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Qarqar</a> in 853 BCE. It later contributed ships to the Persian fleet.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Arwad submitted to Alexander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> in 332 BCE, kept wide autonomy under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and counted its own era from 259 BCE. It lost independence when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> in 64 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arwad.jpg" alt="Arwad" width="320" height="283"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruad_large.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 BCE – 64 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arwad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mattan-Baal, Gerostratus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Phoenician religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Phoenician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arados, Arvad</dd></dl></aside></div>

